Subject: [PATCH v2 2/3] src/lab-process-mail: Thread each revision under the
 one before
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev
Change-Id: qwqznozzqsomwsomoqmsmqvmuszmtxtt
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 08:01:21 +0000

The pass and the mail workflow guide said a revision is a fresh
thread, which no longer matches what format-patch.sh produces. They
now say a revision replies to the previous version, so the maintainer
follows a series and its review in one thread, and that format-patch.sh
picks the reply target, so the agent does not pass its own. The list
researcher is told most earlier versions are now in the same thread.
---
change-id: qwqznozzqsomwsomoqmsmqvmuszmtxtt

 doc/mail-workflow.md           | 10 ++++++----
 src/lab-process-mail/prompt.md | 11 +++++++++--
 2 files changed, 15 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/doc/mail-workflow.md b/doc/mail-workflow.md
index ed9edd89..d5fe46ef 100644
--- a/doc/mail-workflow.md
+++ b/doc/mail-workflow.md
@@ -71,10 +71,12 @@ Conventions:
 
 - One message (or one series) per commit, so the maintainer can accept
   or reject at that granularity.
-- A new revision of a series is a **fresh thread** subject
-  `[PATCH vN]`, with a `v(N-1): <message-id>` link in the cover -- not a
-  reply buried under the old thread. It carries an interdiff against
-  v(N-1) as posted, rebuilt from the list:
+- A new revision of a series, subject `[PATCH vN]`, is a **reply to
+  the previous version's cover** (or only patch), with a
+  `Link to v(N-1): id:<message-id>` line in the cover. Every version
+  and its review stay in one thread, each version beneath the one
+  before; format-patch.sh replies to `--previous`. It carries an
+  interdiff against v(N-1) as posted, rebuilt from the list:
   `libexec/mail/format-patch.sh --previous=<message-id>` requires it.
 - Append; never rewrite already-published history.
 
